{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["A physical instrument has a fixed transduction bandwidth; a checklist can be revised, so the 'cue-set' boundary is endogenous to the evaluation design.","Expert intuition may include configural (non-compensatory) cue combination that a linear checklist cannot encode, even if every cue is present."],"overturn_conditions":["An evaluation in which the checklist is demonstrably broader than the expert's effective cue-set.","A demonstration that configural cues are irrelevant to the specific verifiable task."],"shared_structure":["Both a checklist and a measurement instrument are calibrated against a reference; reliability gain from the instrument is not independent of the reference's coverage.","Using the instrument does not grant the user access to cues the instrument cannot transduce."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_008","SRC_001"],"transfer_claims":["Just as a voltmeter does not reveal a signal outside its bandwidth, a checklist does not reveal a cue outside its authored list.","Panel reliability gain over the expert is bounded by checklist coverage relative to the expert's effective cue-set."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A trained non-expert panel with an explicit checklist will not reliably beat a single expert on verifiable tasks, because a good checklist is a transcript of expert tacit knowledge; if the checklist contains the full cue-set the expert uses, the panel merely parrots the expert (no net gain in expectation); if the checklist omits expert-unique cues, the panel is reliably inferior on exactly the cases that differentiate expertise.","counterconditions":["If the checklist is derived from a strictly larger, validated, external corpus of cues than any single expert possesses, the panel could exceed the expert.","If the expert's intuition has large systematic bias and the checklist comes from a less-biased source, the panel may beat the expert.","If aggregation is across independently authored checklists (not one shared checklist), the logic changes."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Checklists do not create new validity; they externalize and freeze a snapshot of someone's cue-list. The panel's apparent aggregation benefit comes from within-checklist variance reduction, not from independent discovery of the right answer. On verifiable tasks, the hard cases are exactly those where the decisive cue is not on the checklist, and the expert's implicit cue-list is strictly richer. Thus the panel's reliability is a convex combination of (i) easy cases where both succeed and (ii) hard cases where the expert wins; the panel can only match the expert, never exceed, given the same checklist author.","optional_refs":["6882c540f41170da1d2a7326d20a7b1a1e60bd6330f86d72936a5611659de4fd","42336906a5e92d5ff05cfcbbd91b4c5f28139baba2860452910e071fa3a1280e"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.88},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Statistical residuals are computed against a known model; here the 'model' is the checklist and its completeness is unknown.","Panel members are not i.i.d. draws from a fixed model; training and social influence induce correlated deviations."],"overturn_conditions":["Non-listed cues are unknowable or unmeasurable in the evaluation, making the test infeasible.","The panel's training includes implicit access to non-listed cues, invalidating the 'checklist-only' framing."],"shared_structure":["In residual diagnostics, unmodeled structure in errors reveals a missing predictor; here, unmodeled structure in panel errors reveals a missing checklist cue.","A model can score well by matching a proxy that correlates with the target, yet still show systematic error on the proxy-orthogonal subspace."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_006","SRC_007"],"transfer_claims":["Test the panel's errors against non-checklist features exactly as one tests regression residuals against omitted variables.","A flat residual structure is evidence of genuine coverage; a structured residual is evidence of compliance-tracking."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The distinguishing evidence within a single evaluation is the covariance of panel errors with checklist-irrelevant, outcome-relevant features: if checklist compliance tracks the right answer but panel members err together whenever a non-listed cue is decisive, the gain is mere compliance, not genuine reliability.","counterconditions":["If there are no outcome-relevant cues outside the checklist, the test cannot discriminate, and the question reduces to trivial cases.","If panel members have heterogeneous private cues not on the checklist, the structured-error signature may be diluted even without genuine reliability.","If the single expert also fails on non-listed-cue cases, the test no longer separates compliance from expertise."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"If the panel is genuinely more reliable, residual errors should be approximately independent of any outcome-relevant feature the checklist ignores, and should look like uncorrelated noise. If instead the panel's 'success' is compliance-tracking, the panel's errors will cluster systematically on the subset of cases where a non-listed cue is the decisive predictor, yielding a structured error pattern (not noise) with positive correlation across panelists. An error-feature regression on non-listed cues is the discriminating observable: a genuine reliability gain implies zero/near-zero coefficient; compliance-tracking implies a large positive coefficient.","optional_refs":["6882c540f41170da1d2a7326d20a7b1a1e60bd6330f86d72936a5611659de4fd","e990af0e28b4a54bf51333ba24c2f4f90d52881890df3e63f8100b40aa26d821"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.82},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Checklists could in principle encode interactions (branching steps), unlike a strict linear regression.","Human panelists may silently apply configural judgment even when told to be linear, unlike a pure model."],"overturn_conditions":["The checklist is explicitly branching/conditional, encoding interactions.","Empirically, panelists spontaneously violate the linear instruction and apply configural judgment."],"shared_structure":["A linear model forced on a nonlinear process yields systematic error on the interaction subspace, regardless of sample size.","Averaging multiple linear-model predictions does not recover the nonlinear term."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_008"],"transfer_claims":["A flat checklist on a configural task is analogous to fitting a main-effects-only model to data with strong interactions: more data (more panelists) reduces variance but not bias.","The panel's inferiority is a bias problem, not a variance problem, so aggregation cannot fix it."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"There exists a hard boundary class of verifiable tasks where panel+checklist is strictly worse than a single expert: tasks whose correct answer requires configural, non-compensatory cue integration (e.g., recognition-primed decisions where cue X is decisive only in the presence of cue Y, and otherwise should be ignored). A linear checklist forces additive cue evaluation and systematically misweights configural cues, so aggregation cannot rescue it.","counterconditions":["If the checklist is itself configural (e.g., a decision tree with interaction nodes, not a flat list), the argument fails for that checklist.","If the task is purely linear-compensatory, configural misweighting is irrelevant.","If the expert also fails at configural integration, neither approach wins."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Non-compensatory integration means the weight of a cue is contingent on another cue. A checklist that treats each cue as an independent item to be ticked is a linear-compensatory model. On configural tasks, every panelist using the same checklist inherits the same misweighting, so errors are correlated across panelists (systematic, not random), and aggregation does not cancel them. The single expert using intuition can apply the configural rule. Thus the panel's mean judgment is biased in the same direction for every member, and the panel's confidence interval will falsely narrow around the wrong answer.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","42336906a5e92d5ff05cfcbbd91b4c5f28139baba2860452910e071fa3a1280e"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Confidence is self-reported and strategically manipulable; residual structure in errors is behavioral.","Training can teach 'be unsure when the checklist is silent,' producing good calibration without genuine reliability."],"overturn_conditions":["The panel is trained to report low confidence on checklist-silent cases, artifactually passing calibration without genuine knowledge.","Confidence reports are not collected or not trustworthy."],"shared_structure":["Confidence calibration is a classic test of metacognitive access to the decision basis; miscalibration on an unmodeled subspace reveals that confidence is driven by the proxy (checklist completeness), not the target (correctness)."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_006"],"transfer_claims":["Use confidence calibration on the checklist-irrelevant subset as a litmus test: good calibration implies genuine access to the decision basis; poor calibration implies compliance-only."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Within a single evaluation, genuine reliability is distinguished from compliance-tracking by the correlation between panel confidence and correctness on the checklist-irrelevant subset: a genuinely reliable panel should be appropriately calibrated (confidence tracks correctness) even on cases where the checklist gives no guidance, whereas a compliance-tracking panel will be uniformly confident whenever the checklist is complete, independent of whether the checklist-irrelevant cue points the other way.","counterconditions":["If the task has no checklist-irrelevant, outcome-relevant cues, the subset is empty and the test is void.","If the panel is trained to be uncertain when the checklist is silent, the overconfidence signature may vanish without genuine cue-knowledge.","If the expert is equally overconfident, the contrast is lost."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Reliability entails a metacognitive alignment between confidence and accuracy. A compliance-only process produces confidence from checklist completeness ('I checked every box'), which is decoupled from checklist-irrelevant, outcome-relevant signals. Hence on cases where the checklist is silent but an outcome-relevant cue exists, the compliance panel will be overconfident and wrong, producing a miscalibration signature: high confidence, low accuracy, on the checklist-irrelevant subset. A genuinely reliable process would show lower confidence or different accuracy on that subset.","optional_refs":["fbc7c2141d2a36f42c603e1eb13f1a1295c644b1029fc7b27cce33840d2e99d8","e990af0e28b4a54bf51333ba24c2f4f90d52881890df3e63f8100b40aa26d821"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Bias-susceptibility is task- and judge-dependent, unlike a fixed property of a signal.","A checklist can also add valid cues, not only suppress bias, conflating two mechanisms."],"overturn_conditions":["Items cannot be credibly partitioned by bias-susceptibility within the evaluation.","The expert is already debiased by training, leaving no bias for the checklist to suppress."],"shared_structure":["A debiasing instrument helps only on biased judgments; on unbiased judgments it adds noise.","The benefit is conditional on the presence of the failure mode the instrument targets."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_004"],"transfer_claims":["Test panel advantage as an interaction with item bias-susceptibility: significant interaction = genuine debiasing; null interaction with overall advantage = something else (e.g., cue-transfer)."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel+checklist beats the expert only on the subclass of verifiable tasks where expert intuition is corrupted by systematic, domain-general biases (e.g., base-rate neglect, availability) that the checklist mechanically blocks; on bias-free tasks the panel never beats the expert, and within a single evaluation the distinguishing evidence is a task-by-bias-susceptibility interaction: panel advantage should appear only on bias-susceptible items.","counterconditions":["If the expert has no systematic biases on the task domain, the checklist provides no bias-suppression benefit and the panel loses.","If the checklist itself encodes the same bias as the expert (e.g., author bias), no gain.","If the panel members share the expert's bias but the checklist does not suppress it (only lists cues), no gain."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The expert's intuition is a noisy version of a rich cue-set; its dominant failure mode on verifiable tasks is not missing cues but systematic bias (e.g., over-weighting vivid cases). The checklist's value is not adding cues but mechanically enforcing computation that suppresses bias. Therefore the panel's reliability gain is conditional on the task being bias-susceptible. On neutral tasks, the expert's richer cue-set dominates and the panel is equal or worse. A single evaluation can separate these by partitioning items by bias-susceptibility and testing the interaction.","optional_refs":["6882c540f41170da1d2a7326d20a7b1a1e60bd6330f86d72936a5611659de4fd","42336906a5e92d5ff05cfcbbd91b4c5f28139baba2860452910e071fa3a1280e"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Expert intuition is not literally a posterior; the 'generative model' may be unknown.","A checklist could be a perfect algorithm, not a lossy list, in some domains."],"overturn_conditions":["The checklist is an exact algorithm matching the generative model.","The expert's intuition is revealed to be a worse approximation than the checklist (e.g., expert is miscalibrated)."],"shared_structure":["A lossy compression averaged many times converges to the lossy compression, not to the source.","Bias is not reduced by averaging; only variance is."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_002","SRC_003"],"transfer_claims":["Treat the checklist as a lossy codec and the expert as a direct estimator: averaging the codec output cannot recover information the codec discarded.","The signature of lossy compliance is systematic error with high agreement on the codec-divergent subset."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Atypical counterexample: on tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes and a known generative model, a single expert whose intuition is well-calibrated to the generative model can be strictly better than any non-expert panel with a checklist, because the checklist is necessarily a lossy compression of the generative model, whereas the expert's intuition can approximate the posterior directly. The panel's advantage from averaging is a variance reduction that cannot recover the bias introduced by the lossy checklist, and the single evaluation distinguishing evidence is the panel's systematic error on items where the generative model assigns low probability to the checklist-predicted answer.","counterconditions":["If no expert is well-calibrated to the generative model, the counterexample fails.","If the checklist encodes the full generative model (e.g., an algorithm), the panel is not lossy and the argument fails.","If the task is not governed by a compact generative model, the Bayesian framing does not apply."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"If the task has a verifiable generative structure P(outcome | features), an expert with well-calibrated intuition approximates the Bayesian posterior. A checklist encodes a discrete proxy rule, which is a lossy compression of P. The panel averages multiple applications of the same proxy, reducing variance but preserving proxy bias. On items where the proxy and P diverge, the panel is systematically wrong with high agreement (false precision). The expert, approximating P directly, is better. The distinguishing evidence is the panel's systematic error on the proxy-divergent subset, coupled with high inter-rater agreement: high agreement + high error on a defined subset = compliance tracking a lossy proxy, not genuine reliability.","optional_refs":["6882c540f41170da1d2a7326d20a7b1a1e60bd6330f86d72936a5611659de4fd","fbc7c2141d2a36f42c603e1eb13f1a1295c644b1029fc7b27cce33840d2e99d8"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.35}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[{"direction":"directed","from_ref":"NEW_001","holds_when":"Checklist authorship is endogenous (improvable by feedback) but checklist structure remains linear.","relation_hint":"NEW_002 is a counterfactual challenge to the 'lossy compression' mechanism in NEW_001: crowd-evolved checklists could reduce bias, but configural integration remains a separate failure mode.","to_ref":"NEW_002","weakens_when":"Checklists are branching/configural, closing the integration gap."}],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_006","SRC_008"],"content":"Speculative synthesis of the counterexample-first stance: The recurring structure across candidates is a bias-variance decomposition applied to the panel-vs-expert comparison. The checklist is a fixed estimator (low variance, bias determined by author's cue-set). The expert is a high-variance, potentially low-bias estimator (if intuition is rich). Aggregation (panel) reduces variance but not bias. Therefore panel>expert iff checklist bias < expert variance. This is a clean, criticizable frame. The distinguishing evidence within one evaluation is anything that reveals the bias component: structured residuals on non-listed cues, miscalibration on checklist-silent cases, or systematic error on proxy-divergent items. All candidates are instances of 'find the bias'.","possible_next_move":"Formalize the bias-variance decomposition for panel+checklist vs expert and derive the exact conditions under which panel>expert in expectation, including the interaction with task difficulty.","unfinished":"The frame assumes checklist bias and expert variance are separable and measurable; this may fail if the expert's 'intuition' is itself a biased checklist, conflating the two estimators.","why_keep_this":"It unifies the five candidates under one bias-variance frame and names the common evidentiary target: the bias component. Future turns can attack the frame or instantiate it."},"local_key":"NEW_001"},{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_003"],"content":"Counterfactual: What if the checklist is not authored by an expert but crowd-evolved (e.g., Delphi with feedback on verifiable outcomes)? Then the 'checklist is a lossy compression of the expert' objection weakens: the checklist could exceed any single expert's cue-set. But the compliance-tracking problem remains: panelists may comply with a rich checklist yet still fail on cases requiring configural integration. So even a crowd-evolved checklist does not guarantee genuine reliability; it only shifts the bias boundary. The distinguishing evidence would still be structured errors on interaction-heavy items.","possible_next_move":"Specify the minimal configural task that a flat checklist cannot solve and test whether panelists spontaneously add configural judgment.","why_keep_this":"It blocks the easy refutation that a better-authored checklist solves the problem, by showing the configural-integration issue is orthogonal to authorship."},"local_key":"NEW_002"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Can the bias-variance frame be empirically instantiated in a single evaluation without knowing the true generative model, or does it require oracle access to decompose bias from variance?","related_refs":[]},{"question":"Are there verifiable tasks where configural integration is the dominant difficulty and flat checklists are the only practical tool, making the panel's failure inevitable?","related_refs":[]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}